## Full Eternity vs Seven Stone vs Half-Eternity

[![Asscher cut lab-grown diamond eternity band in rose gold 4ct showing continuous step-cut windows at full band width](/images/asscher-cut-eternity-band-rose-gold-4ct.jpg)](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings?stoneShape=asscher&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

**Full Eternity Band:** Asscher step-cut stones run all the way around the band — the Hall of Corridors effect is continuous and visible from every angle, not just the top. Cannot be resized; the most visually complete asscher eternity format. Range in this catalog: 2ct–10ct.

**Seven Stone Band:** Seven asscher step-cut stones are set across the top of the band — the Hall of Corridors effect is visible from the front-facing view, which is how the ring is seen in most contexts. The metal shank underneath allows limited resizing (±0.5 size); the most practical format for buyers uncertain about their long-term size. Range: 1ct–3.5ct.

**Which to choose:**

Choose the full eternity if you know your exact ring size, you want the asscher step-cut visible from every angle, and you want the full Hall of Corridors effect at its most complete. The full eternity is the statement format.

Choose the seven stone if you have any uncertainty about ring size, you want a band that can be adjusted if needed, or you want the asscher look in a slightly less dramatic width. Seven stones of lab F-G/VS2-SI1 across the top creates the same visual impact as a full eternity at the front view.

Farzana's guidance: "Most buyers come in thinking they want the full eternity because they have seen it in pictures. I ask two questions: do you know your size exactly, and do you plan to stack this with your engagement ring? If the answer to either is 'not sure,' I steer them to the seven stone — you can always stack a seven stone and adjust the fit, but you cannot unstick a full eternity that doesn't fit."

## Metal Choice for Asscher Cut Eternity Bands

**14K White Gold:** Highest color visibility format — the Eternity Color Floor applies in full, and H-I color reads as a continuous warm stripe across all stones. F-G lab is the only color grade that meets the floor in white gold; white gold needs rhodium replating every 18-24 months for an eternity band.

**Platinum:** Slightly warmer in tone than rhodium-plated white gold, but still a white metal — the Eternity Color Floor applies almost identically. Platinum does not need replating; the natural patina develops over time and can be polished professionally, making the maintenance advantage over white gold meaningful for daily wear. Natural platinum eternity bands are available at $100-350 premium over white gold per size.

**14K Yellow Gold:** The warm metal baseline partially absorbs H-I color warmth — the Eternity Color Floor is lower here, and G-H natural is acceptable where H-I is problematic in white metal. Lab F-G in yellow gold reads as cleanly colorless against the warm setting; yellow gold does not show scratches as visibly as white gold and does not need replating. A strong practical choice.

**14K Rose Gold:** Similar to yellow gold for the Eternity Color Floor — warm metal reduces H-I visibility, and natural H-I in rose gold is more tolerable than in white settings. Lab F-G/VS2-SI1 in rose gold creates a warm, romantic pairing; rose gold is not available in the natural full eternity catalog (natural platinum eternity is the white metal alternative).

## Stacking the Asscher Eternity Band

An asscher cut eternity band is most commonly worn stacked with an asscher cut engagement ring. The step-cut geometry of both pieces creates a continuous window effect from the engagement ring center stone through the eternity band stones.

For the stack to work cleanly, the eternity band needs to sit flush against the engagement ring. Wide-base solitaire settings or settings with a high profile can create a gap between the two rings — the eternity band and engagement ring should be tried together when possible before purchasing.

For sizing: size for the rings together, not each ring individually. Two rings together on the finger require slightly more space than each ring alone — typically 0.25-0.5 size larger than your single-ring size. This is part of Mistake 2 from the TL;DR: buyers who size for the engagement ring alone and then add an eternity band often find the combined stack is too tight.

