## Frequently Asked Questions

### Can an asscher cut eternity band be resized?

A full eternity band (stones all the way around) cannot be resized — the stones circle the entire ring and there is no plain metal section to cut and solder for adjustment. A seven stone band with a plain metal shank can sometimes be resized by ±0.5 size maximum; buyers who need sizing flexibility should choose the seven stone format and size precisely before purchase.

### What is the Eternity Color Floor for an asscher cut eternity band?

The Eternity Color Floor is the minimum color grade that reads as consistently white across all stones in the band simultaneously. For white gold or platinum: F-G minimum; for yellow gold or rose gold: G-H acceptable. H-I color in white metal reads as a continuous warm band across all 7-15 step-cut windows — the color warmth compounds across adjacent stones rather than appearing as a single isolated warm stone.

### What is the Hall of Corridors effect?

The Hall of Corridors describes the visual depth created when multiple adjacent asscher step-cut stones are set in an eternity band. Each stone's Hall of Mirrors reflects the neighboring stones' step-cut facets — the result is a continuous geometric corridor of depth that runs across the full width of the band. Round brilliant eternity bands scatter light into sparkle; asscher eternity bands create a geometric corridor of depth that is unique to the step-cut format.

### Why is lab better than natural for asscher cut eternity bands?

Lab asscher eternity bands grade F-G/VS2-SI1 — meeting the Eternity Color Floor for all metal settings; natural bands grade H-I/SI1-SI2 — falling below the floor in white gold and platinum settings. Lab also costs significantly less: lab 3ct WG = $3,060 vs natural 3ct WG = $8,580, a $5,520 difference for stones that grade better on both color and clarity. There is no quality advantage to natural in the asscher eternity band catalog.

### What is the difference between a full eternity band and a seven stone band?

A full eternity band has asscher step-cut stones set all the way around the ring; a seven stone band has seven stones across the top with a plain metal shank on the sides and bottom. Full eternity cannot be resized; seven stone can be sized ±0.5 — and full eternity shows the Hall of Corridors from every angle, while seven stone shows it only from the front-facing view. Both create the step-cut geometric effect in the visible portion.

### How do I size an asscher cut eternity band if I plan to stack it?

Size with both the eternity band and the engagement ring on simultaneously — not each ring alone, since two rings together require approximately 0.25-0.5 size more than a single ring. Buyers who size for the engagement ring alone and add an eternity band often find the combined stack is too tight; this is the most common sizing mistake in the eternity band category.

### What metal is best for an asscher cut eternity band?

For lab F-G/VS2-SI1 bands: 14K white gold or platinum shows the clean color most effectively. For natural H-I/SI1-SI2 bands: 14K yellow gold or rose gold is the better choice — warm metal partially absorbs H-I color warmth across the step-cut windows; platinum adds a maintenance advantage (no rhodium replating) and is worth the small premium over white gold for daily wear.

### What total carat weight should I choose for an asscher eternity band?

For a subtle wedding band that complements an engagement ring: 1-2ct (seven stone format). For a statement band that is visually prominent on its own: 3-5ct; for maximum presence as a solo architectural ring: 5-10ct. At 5ct, the lab full eternity costs $3,876 in white gold — still dramatically less than natural at any size, and the carat weight also determines the visual width of the band.

### How does an asscher eternity band compare to a round brilliant eternity band?

Round brilliant eternity bands create sparkle from every viewing angle — the brilliant facets scatter light in all directions; asscher eternity bands create geometric depth, with the Hall of Corridors visible from the front-facing view. Round brilliant bands forgive lower color and clarity grades; asscher eternity bands require higher grades due to the Eternity Color Floor. Asscher eternity bands are considerably rarer and more architecturally distinctive.

### Is a bezel-set asscher eternity ring different from a prong-set one?

Yes — bezel-set asscher eternity rings have a thin metal rim around each stone rather than four prongs; the bezel adds protection and gives the band a more modern, enclosed appearance. The bezel slightly reduces the Hall of Corridors visibility by framing each stone individually, while prong-set bands maximize the step-cut window openness. Bezel is the right choice for active lifestyles where prong protection is a concern.

### Do the Bella Vaughan asscher bands use higher quality stones?

Bella Vaughan bands are designer-priced and use natural diamonds, but the quality grades are not always specified explicitly in the listing titles. These bands command a premium for the design aesthetic — the architectural pavé settings, the 18K gold alloys, and the brand identity — rather than for published stone quality tiers. For known published quality grades (F-G/VS2-SI1 for lab, H-I/SI1-SI2 for natural), the standard Blue Nile eternity and seven stone bands are the reference.

### Can I wear an asscher cut eternity band on its own without an engagement ring?

Yes — asscher cut eternity bands and seven stone bands work as standalone rings; the Hall of Corridors effect is fully visible with or without an adjacent engagement ring. Many buyers wear the seven stone or full eternity as an anniversary ring, a right-hand ring, or a standalone wedding band without a solitaire. The lab full eternity at 3-5ct is particularly strong as a standalone piece — the continuous step-cut depth creates a visual impact that does not require a center stone.

### What is the most important thing to know before buying an asscher eternity band?

Know your exact ring size before purchasing — not your estimated size, not the size of your engagement ring, but your actual measured finger size for the specific band. If you plan to stack it with an engagement ring, size for the stack. And choose lab or choose yellow/rose gold for natural: H-I color in a white metal asscher eternity band is the most common buyer regret in the entire asscher jewelry category.

### How do I clean an asscher cut eternity band?

Clean with warm water, mild dish soap, and a soft toothbrush — work gently around the stone settings, especially under the bezels or between prongs where debris accumulates; rinse thoroughly and dry with a soft cloth. Professional cleaning once or twice per year is recommended. For white gold eternity bands, monitor the rhodium plating — when the band begins to show yellow at contact points, it is time to replate; platinum bands need professional polishing periodically but no replating.
